# Searching for many keys at once: `Str\Matcher` and `Str\Replacer`

[PR #25122]({{PR_URL}}) proposes two classes for `ext/standard`, plus `Str\MatchResult` for results; they aren't in a released PHP yet.
They find or replace many literal keys in one pass over the text:

```php
$matcher = new Str\Matcher(['viagra', 'casino', 'free money']);
$matcher->matches($comment);   // also getMatchedKeywords(), countMatches(), matchAll()
(new Str\Replacer(['str_contains' => '<a href="…">str_contains</a>']))->replace($page);
```

The report compares them with the fastest existing way to do the same: a `str_contains()`/`strpos()` loop, `strtr()`, or a PCRE alternation.

Two modes:

- **Prepared**: a call on an object built earlier, once per worker in a long-running runtime or once before a loop over many texts. Under PHP-FPM an object lives for one request.
- **One-shot**: building the object and one call.

Main results, times with GCC:

- A prepared object with 32 keys or more is {{PREPARED_32_TIMES}} times faster per call.
- One call on a short text is slower: on 128 B with 16 random keys the build and call take {{ONESHOT_128B_16_NATIVE}} µs, the fastest existing method {{ONESHOT_128B_16_REF}} µs.
- One call pays for the build on longer texts: at 4 KiB with 128 random keys it changes the time by {{ONESHOT_4K_128}}. On whole manual pages of {{PAGES_LONGEST}}, one auto-linking call with 128 or 512 names changes it by {{PAGES_LONG_SMALL}}.
- One `replace()` with a large dictionary is slower than `strtr()`: {{REPLACE_1024_SHORT}} with 1024 random keys on 1–4 KiB, {{PAGES_REPLACE_3345}} with the 3,345 auto-linking names on pages of 1–16 KiB.
- `matches()` stops at the first match. Its time grows with the position of the match, not with the number of keys. With a match in the middle of 4 KiB a prepared call takes {{ANY_MID_16_US}} µs with 16 keys and {{ANY_MID_1024_US}} µs with 1024, the fastest existing method {{REF_MID_16_US}} and {{REF_MID_1024_US}} µs.
- The ranges above leave out a match in the first 64 bytes. There `matches()` and PCRE stop after a few bytes. Prepared, the new class takes {{START_NATIVE_16_US}} µs at any key count, PCRE {{START_PCRE_LOW_US}} µs with 16 keys and {{START_PCRE_HIGH_US}} µs with 1024: the change is {{START_16}} with 16 keys and {{START_1024}} with 1024. One-shot, the new class still builds its object, {{START_ONESHOT_NATIVE_US}} µs. PCRE only builds its pattern string, {{START_ONESHOT_PCRE_US}} µs, and keeps the compiled pattern in its cache. One call changes the time by {{ONESHOT_START_16}}.

Detailed tables by question: [Where to look](#where-to-look).

## Application scenarios

{{SCENARIO_INPUTS}}

- **Moderation**: the literal substring check of moderation. [Civil Comments](https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/civil_comments) against the English [LDNOOBW](dictionaries/ldnoobw/ATTRIBUTION.md) list, ignoring ASCII case. No word boundaries, Unicode case folding or judgement of false positives.
- **Bot detection**: the literal patterns of [crawler-user-agents](https://github.com/monperrus/crawler-user-agents) against real User-Agent strings, mixed by the generator: 80% browsers picked by their observed weight in the source data, 20% crawler examples. The 10,000 texts hold {{BOTS_DISTINCT}} distinct strings; caching results per string is not modelled.
- **Auto-linking**: PHP manual pages as plain text, markup removed; `replace()` turns every function name that contains `_` into a link. Names without `_`, such as `strlen`, are not in the list. Existing links and identifier boundaries are not handled. With 3,345 names only `strtr()` competes for `replace()`: PCRE can't compile a replacement pattern this large.

Times are GCC, change is GCC / Clang. A time is the time of a block of calls over the pool divided by the calls, not the latency of a median text. The last four rows are pages of 4–16 KiB, see [One call on long pages](#one-call-on-long-pages).
Calls to repay the build: 1 when a single call is already faster. Otherwise an estimate: build time ÷ prepared saving per call, with the existing method's one-time work counted as zero, which favours it.

{{SCENARIOS_TABLE}}

### One call on long pages

The auto-linking task on whole manual pages, grouped by length. The dictionary is the full list of 3,345 names or a random subset of 128 or 512 of them. {{PAGES_INPUTS}} Pages under 1 KiB and the 9 over 16 KiB are left out. {{PAGES_HITS}}

Build and one call, change against the fastest existing method, GCC / Clang:

{{PAGES_TABLE}}

With 3,345 names building the object takes most of the time of `replace()`, and `strtr()` stays faster. These cells ran in a separate series with the same builds: [data/pages.json](data/pages.json).

## Results by keys and text length

Each value is the change in time per call against the fastest existing method for the same call, text, keys and mode, GCC / Clang. Which methods compete: [How it was compared](#how-it-was-compared); the time of each of them for every value: [all-methods.md](all-methods.md).
Negative = the new class is faster: −50% is twice as fast, −90% ten times. **Bold**: faster by more than 3% with both compilers. † = noisy value (see [METHOD.md](METHOD.md)).
Random keys are random strings of the letters a–p, written into English text. The text has one match in the middle third, or none where the row says so. `matches()` stops at the match, about halfway. The other calls read the whole text.

